## 7. Use case 1: govern a payment chain (2:30) [C]
1. Concierge chat: *"A retail customer wants to send a $2,000 payment from their chequing account. Coordinate it end to end."*
   → Concierge → Retail → Payments → Ledger. Point at the "Transferring control to agent" lines.
2. **Agent Trail**: each hop, the logged-in user, Permit, which policy.
3. **Gateway Policies** → `northbridge-policy`: "default-deny; only these lanes. Agents never hold the Gemini key — the gateway does."
4. Add the forbid (cheat sheet). "A forbid always beats a permit."
5. Wait ~60 s → **Access Map**: Retail → Payments line gone. "Payments disappears from Retail's world." → re-run: stops at Retail.
6. Delete the forbid.

## 8. Use case 2: stop a rogue agent (2:00) [C][D]
> "Shadows never use the gateway, so gateway policy can't touch them. This is where the kernel layer comes in."
1. `k -n tigera-lynx exec deploy/shadow-agent-2 -- ls /` → works.
2. Discover → shadow-agent-2 → **Quarantine**. Wait ~10 s.
3. Same command → `operation not permitted`. `k -n tigera-lynx logs deploy/shadow-agent-2 --tail=5` → `sleep: Operation not permitted`.
> "No new programs, no file writes, no outbound network — a freeze, not a kill." [D]
4. Lift quarantine. *(Optional, 45 s)* apply the sandbox policy: 8.8.8.8 works, 1.1.1.1 blocked.
> "Quarantine is the emergency brake; a sandbox policy is the seatbelt."

## 9. Service identities (0:30) — Register → Service Identities [D]
> "Not every caller is an agent. A nightly script or a Slack bot gets a **service identity**: it can call agents, but nobody calls it.
> Same rule — default-deny until a policy allows it."

## 10. What's new in 1.5 (0:30) — one slide [RN]
Source: docs.tigera.io/lynx/release-notes, v1.5.0, released Nov 9, 2026. **This cluster runs 1.4.2: mention only, don't demo.**

Slide bullets:
- **LLM routes**: agents call one name; the gateway picks the provider, and fails over if one goes down.
- **Events**: one page for denied calls and newly discovered agents.
- **Local user accounts**: sign in without an identity provider (break-glass admin).

> "Lynx 1.5 shipped last week. Three things worth knowing: **LLM routes** — agents call one name, and the gateway picks
> Gemini, OpenAI or Anthropic behind it, and fails over if one goes down. **Events** — every denied call and every newly
> discovered agent, in one place. And **local accounts**, so you can sign in without an identity provider."

*(Optional extra line, from 1.4.0, "Coding Sessions (alpha)": "Even a Claude Code session outside the cluster can be admitted as a caller, with the person behind it on the Agent Trail." Mention only; off by default.)*

## 11. Wrap-up (0:30) [1P]
> "Agentic AI acts on its own — so every call needs an identity and a decision. Lynx puts a gateway in the path, finds the agents that skip it with eBPF,
> contains them in the kernel, and records every decision. One Helm chart, any CNI, self-hosted — built by the team behind Calico."

